The unmodified Vallado SGP4 kernel in third_party/vallado is provided under
the unrestricted-use exception documented in third_party/vallado/NOTICE.
Its upstream author and copyright notices are preserved in the source files.
The separately maintained scalar-templated adaptation in third_party/vallado_ad
derives from that same permitted source and retains its attribution.

Public verification fixtures in tests/data are from python-sgp4, distributed
under the MIT license reproduced in tests/data/python-sgp4-LICENSE.

Build dependencies retain their own licenses: Ceres Solver (BSD-3-Clause,
reproduced in third_party/ceres-LICENSE), Eigen (MPL-2.0 and notices),
glog (BSD-3-Clause, reproduced in third_party/glog-LICENSE), nlohmann/json
(MIT), GoogleTest (BSD), and CPM.cmake (MIT). The optional CLI additionally
uses spdlog (MIT, reproduced in third_party/spdlog-LICENSE).
See docs/dependencies.md for versions, provenance, and distribution details.

The independent ISS fixture is a public NASA/JSC/FOD/TOPO forecast, with
original attribution and bytes retained. The converted TEME CSV identifies
watson's conversion and NASA's source data. The IANA-distributed leap-second
table retains its original attribution. See tests/data/independent/README.md.

Optional fixture conversion uses ERFA (BSD) through a pinned CPM archive;
it is not linked into the installed library. The separately built Project
Pluto reference executable and its GPL sources are not distributed here or
linked into orbit-fit. Numerical comparison outputs retain provenance in
qualification/README.md. The published Vallado frame reference values were
consulted through Orekit's Apache-2.0 test corpus; no Orekit code is included.
